WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.725
Bow and beam is one case of a more general trick, called doubling the angle on the bow.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:14.575
Whenever the angle on the bow doubles, the distance you ran between the two bearings equals your distance from the object at the second bearing.

3
00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:16.600
Go down the table.

4
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.400
Forty-five degrees, then ninety.

5
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.550
That’s bow and beam.

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.975
Distance off abeam equals distance run.

7
00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.575
Thirty degrees, then sixty.

8
00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:31.650
Distance off at the second bearing equals distance run.

9
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.725
Twenty-two and a half, then forty-five.

10
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:36.700
Here’s a bonus.

11
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:42.775
Your distance off when you finally pass abeam will be about seven tenths of the distance run.

12
00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:44.925
Why is that useful?

13
00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:50.825
It tells you early, well before you get there, whether you’re going to pass too close.

14
00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:54.925
And the general rule: any angle, then twice that angle.

15
00:00:55.175 --> 00:00:59.000
Distance off at the second bearing equals distance run.
